I wrote `verification-repair.json` with status **insufficient_evidence**, so the Dan Ives page stays on hold. I changed no other files and published nothing. It passes the schema check, and a dry run of the release gate blocks it as expected.

**The facts hold up.** I fetched fresh copies of all 40 written sources the draft cites. Every quote matched its source, and the ones I read in full context were used fairly. For the three podcasts, I downloaded the audio and transcribed it myself; those quotes match too. The SEC order and the Barron's conflict-of-interest story both give each side, with dates and attribution. All 52 editing decisions hold up against their tests, including the one rejection: the draft is still about 700 words over target, which I accepted but took into account in the writing score. All 17 passages the reviewers asked to keep survived.

**The hold is about the type call.** The article itself says Type 7 vs Type 3 is "close," and I agree no sourced evidence separates them. The Seven case rests on how he describes his own motives, mostly in friendly or promotional interviews. Those same interviews also talk about how the clothes look to investors and about the brand, which points toward Three. A 25-year public record also rules out the "open case" format.

